### 3b. Properties + Commercial — step-by-step routing (owner asked, 2026-09-13)

Three Properties-tenant mailboxes must reach the hub, **both directions** (received *and* sent):
`info@fishboneproperties.co.uk`, `irina@fishboneproperties.co.uk`, `commercial@fishboneproperties.co.uk`.
All three live in the **Properties Google Workspace** (Commercial rides inside Properties' tenant), which
is a **different tenant** from the Construction hub — so this is cross-tenant delivery, and Peter must
sort `info@`/`irina@` → `[FP]` and `commercial@` → `[FM]` by `Delivered-To`.

Key fact that drives the design: **Gmail's per-user "Forward a copy of incoming mail" forwards INBOUND
mail only.** It never copies what the mailbox *sends*. Sent mail is the evidence a task was actioned, so
capturing it needs an **Admin-console routing rule** (exactly like Construction's live "Ops routing").
So we do it in two parts. Part A (routing rule) alone can actually cover both directions; Part B is the
simpler per-mailbox alternative for the inbound half if you'd rather not filter inbound in the rule.

**Prerequisite:** the `ops@fishboneconstruction.co.uk` → hub forward already exists; and the
half-built `ops@fishboneproperties.co.uk → ops@fishboneconstruction.co.uk` chain means you *can*
consolidate Properties internally first and cross the tenant boundary once. Either shape works; steps
below deliver **straight to `ops@fishboneconstruction.co.uk`** for clarity.

#### Part A — one Admin-console routing rule (captures SENT + received in one place) — recommended

Do this in the **Properties** Workspace Admin console (admin.google.com, signed in as a Properties
super-admin — *not* Construction's), mirroring Construction's "Ops routing":

1. **Apps → Google Workspace → Gmail → Routing.** Click **Configure** / **Add another rule**.
2. Name it **`Ops routing → Peter hub`**.
3. **Email messages to affect:** tick **Outbound**, **Internal – sending**, and **Inbound** +
   **Internal – receiving**. (Outbound + Internal-sending = the sent-mail half; Inbound +
   Internal-receiving = the received half. Ticking all four makes this one rule do both directions.)
4. **For the above types of messages, do the following → Add more recipients → Add.**
5. In the recipient panel choose **Advanced** and set **Envelope filter → Only affect specific
   envelope senders → matches this pattern (regexp):**
   `^(info|irina|commercial)@fishboneproperties\.co\.uk$`
   (This is what limits the rule to the three mailboxes and, on the outbound side, keys on the *sender*
   so only their sent mail is copied.)
6. Under **Also deliver to → Add more recipients**, add **`ops@fishboneconstruction.co.uk`**.
7. Leave "Do not deliver spam" as default; **Show options → do not** rewrite the To/From; save.
8. **Bypass the spam/relay block for cross-tenant delivery:** because the copy is sent from Properties'
   tenant to a Construction address, add `fishboneconstruction.co.uk` (or the specific hub address) to
   Properties' **Gmail → Routing → allowed senders / bypass** if the test copy lands in spam.

#### Part B — per-mailbox inbound forward (only if you skip the Inbound half of Part A)

For **each** of `info@`, `irina@`, `commercial@`, signed in as that user (or via Admin → user →
"Sign in as"): **Gmail → Settings (gear) → See all settings → Forwarding and POP/IMAP →
Add a forwarding address → `ops@fishboneconstruction.co.uk`.** Google emails a **confirmation code** to
the hub — **Minda opens `ops@fishboneconstruction.co.uk`, reads the code, and confirms** (Peter must not;
read-only). Then select **"Forward a copy of incoming mail to `ops@…` and keep Gmail's copy in the
Inbox."** Save. (Admin can force-approve the forwarding address at **Apps → Gmail → End User Access →
Automatic forwarding** if the per-user confirmation is disabled.)

#### Verify (each address, both directions)

- **Received:** send a test *to* `info@`, `irina@`, `commercial@` in turn → each must appear in
  `ops@fishboneconstruction.co.uk` with `Delivered-To: <that address>` (Peter keys on this).
- **Sent:** *from* each of the three, send a test to an external address → the copy must also land in
  the hub (this proves the routing-rule sent-mail half; per-user forwarding will NOT show this).
- Confirm the hub still shows **no personal mail** (access is Minda + Peter only, §6).

Filed as an **Eugene runbook** at execution time (`Runbook-Properties-Commercial-Routing-to-Hub.md`);
Minda executes every console step, Eugene/Peter verify read-only.

